2.3    Exogenous processes

A key determinant of banks’ choices with respect to asset and liability structure is the uncertainty
they face with respect to returns on loans and liquid assets, prices of liquid assets and interest
rates on long- and short-term debt. We assume that price processes on the asset and liability
side are exogenously determined, i.e. banks are price takers whose decisions do not have any
impact on asset returns. That is, rates on loans and liquid assets are determined in competitive
markets, and banks can (up to a certain limit) expand their lending at the prevailing market
rates. This assumption is justified by the short- to medium perspective taken by our partial
equilibrium model: while one would expect some interaction between loan volumes and interest
rates in a long-term general equilibrium framework, we think that it is unlikely that banks
account for such long-run effects in their short-term adjustment decisions.

